␀␀%l:%M %p␀␀␀␀%A %b %e␀␀␀␀shown␀␀␀Restarting: the radio can only be started once per boot␀editor: internal=%uKB largest=%uKB dma_largest=%uKB
␀␀␀␀Carefree␀␀␀␀Compound␀␀␀␀USE MY NETWORK␀␀USE OWN HOTSPOT␀The last attempt could not reach the network.␀␀␀The radio has had its one session this boot; restart the console to sync again.␀A check is due: press SYNC NOW, or open the Wi-Fi editor, which syncs too.␀␀Checked whenever the Wi-Fi editor is open, or on SYNC NOW.␀␀SYNC NOW␀␀␀␀SYNCING...␀␀TAP AGAIN TO RESET␀␀RESET SCORES␀␀␀␀LIGHT MODE␀␀DARK MODE␀␀␀FAST CHARGE: ON␀FAST CHARGE: OFF␀␀␀␀PAD POWER: IN GAMES␀PAD POWER: ON BATTERY␀␀␀PAD POWER: OFF␀␀USB-C DATA: OFF UNLESS BOOTED ON A COMPUTER␀USB-C DATA: ALWAYS ON␀␀␀Leave this game?␀␀␀␀Scores for the current match will be lost.␀␀KEEP PLAYING␀␀␀␀QUIT TO MENU␀␀␀␀CONTENT EDITOR␀␀Joining the network...␀␀Join this Wi-Fi network from a phone␀␀␀␀basic_string: construction from null is not valid␀␀␀No password. The editor should open by itself;␀␀if it doesn't, open  http://%s/␀Open this on a phone or laptop␀␀or  http://tabulous5.local/␀Games reload their word packs when you finish.␀␀Could not start␀Check the Wi-Fi details in include/secrets.h␀␀␀␀FINISH␀␀SETTINGS␀␀␀␀DONE␀␀␀␀POWER OFF␀␀␀CONSOLE␀TIME & SCORES␀␀␀TIME␀␀␀␀%A, %B %e␀␀␀Not set yet␀Joining the network to ask the time...␀␀Asking the network for the time...␀␀%b %e at %l:%M %p␀␀␀at  ␀␀␀␀Set from the network on %s; the clock chip keeps it between boots.␀␀The clock appears once the network has set it, over Wi-Fi.␀␀TIME ZONE␀␀␀<␀␀␀HIGH SCORES␀DISPLAY␀Light mode is for playing outdoors.␀VOLUME␀␀-␀␀␀+␀␀␀%u%%␀␀␀␀EDIT CONTENT OVER WI-FI␀POWER␀␀␀Plug chargers in from this menu. To use a computer, press the button once␀␀␀(a reset) with it attached. Pad power on a charger shuts the charger off.␀␀␀GAMES␀␀␀tap to show or hide, drag the handle to reorder␀BACK TO GAME␀␀␀␀TAB␀U␀␀␀LOUS␀␀␀␀PhraseCraze␀Describe it. Don't get caught holding it.␀␀␀Two teams. Pick a category, then describe the phrase on screen
without saying any word in it. When your team guesses, tap
anywhere to pass the device to the other team.

A hidden timer beeps faster and faster. Whoever is holding it when
the buzzer sounds gives the other team a point - and that team then
gets one guess at the phrase for a bonus point. First to 7 wins.

SKIP changes the phrase. Each team has its own skips.

In SETTINGS: scoring (classic, or a point per phrase), round length,
difficulty including a kids mode, skips, points to win, and which
word packs to use. Tap a team's card to rename it.␀FiveHead␀␀␀␀On your forehead. Tilt to score.␀␀␀␀One player holds the device against their forehead, screen facing
everyone else. They describe or act out the phrase; the holder
guesses it.

Tilt the device DOWN to score it, UP to pass. Both are recorded and
you see the whole list at the end of the round. A visible clock runs
the turn, then it passes to the other team.

The GOT IT and PASS buttons do the same thing if tilting isn't
convenient.

In SETTINGS: round length, turns per team, difficulty, which word
packs to use, and tilt direction and sensitivity.␀Minesweeper␀Dig, flag, don't guess.␀Tap a square to dig. A number says how many mines touch that
square. Clear every square that isn't a mine to win.

HOLD a square to flag it as a mine. The TAP = DIG / TAP = FLAG
button swaps what a plain tap does, if you'd rather not hold.

Tap a number that already has all its mines flagged and it opens
everything else around it.

The first tap of a game is always safe, and so is everything
immediately around it - you always get a region to reason from.

The level button cycles Easy, Medium and Hard. Harder means more
mines, not a bigger board: squares stay big enough to tap.␀Solitaire␀␀␀Klondike. Tap a card, tap where it goes.␀␀␀␀Klondike. Build the four foundations up from ace to king in suit,
and you have won.

TAP a card to pick it up, then tap where it should go. Tap it again
to put it back down. A run of face-up cards moves together if it is
already in order - descending, alternating colours.

Columns build DOWN in alternating colours; only a king starts an
empty column. Turning over a face-down card happens automatically.

Tap the stock (top left) to deal. When it runs out, tap again to
turn the waste back over.

AUTO sends every card it legally can to the foundations - use it to
finish a won game without tapping fifty times. UNDO steps back
through your last moves.

DRAW 1 / DRAW 3 switches how many cards the stock turns at once.
Draw 3 is the harder classic, and each is scored separately.␀␀␀Sudoku␀␀Nine by nine. No guessing needed.␀␀␀Fill the grid so that every row, every column and every 3x3 box
contains 1 to 9 exactly once.

Tap a square, then tap a number. Tapping the same number again
clears it. NOTES writes small pencil marks instead of an answer.

The selected square's row, column and box are shaded, along with
every square holding the same digit. A number that repeats in a
row, column or box turns red.

Every puzzle is generated with exactly one solution, so it can
always be finished by logic - you never have to guess.

The level button cycles Easy, Medium, Hard and Expert, which start
you with fewer and fewer clues.␀␀␀Gamepad Test␀␀␀␀On-screen NES pad. Hold it and see how it feels.␀␀␀␀A test rig, not a game. It draws the on-screen controller an NES
emulator would use and lights each control while you hold it, so the
layout can be judged before there is anything to play.

The picture area shows the eight buttons in the order the NES itself
reports them, plus the raw byte a core would be handed.

Several controls work at once - hold a direction and press A. The
corners of the pad give diagonals.

It sits at 2x (512x480) because that leaves 33 mm either side for the
controls. At 3x the picture is bigger but the columns shrink to 22 mm,
which is too narrow for a thumb.

Hide it from SETTINGS when it is in the way.␀␀␀NES␀Every cartridge on the card, with sound.␀␀␀␀Runs NES ROMs from /nes on a FAT32 microSD card. A few can be built in
instead: put .nes files in data/nes and flash them with
pio run -e tab5 -t uploadfs

Pick a ROM from the list. Anything it cannot run is still listed, but
dimmed and labelled with the reason - usually a memory mapper the core
does not implement. NROM, UxROM, MMC1 and MMC3 are supported.

The list is grouped: Favourites, then # and A to Z down the left. The
star on a row keeps a game in Favourites.

2x TOUCH plays with the on-screen pad. 3x GAMEPAD fills the height and
expects a USB gamepad; hold SELECT and START together to come back.

ROMs are yours to supply. None are shipped with this device.␀␀␀Game Boy␀␀␀␀The 1989 handheld, in its own green.␀␀␀␀Runs Game Boy ROMs from /gb on a FAT32 microSD card. A few can be built
in instead: put .gb files in data/gb and flash them with
pio run -e tab5 -t uploadfs

Game Boy Color cartridges are a different machine and are not supported.
Anything the core cannot run is still listed, dimmed, with the reason.

3x TOUCH plays with the on-screen pad. 5x GAMEPAD fills the height
exactly - every Game Boy pixel becomes five - and expects a USB gamepad;
hold SELECT and START together to come back.

Games that saved to a battery-backed cartridge save here too, to a .sav
file beside the ROM, written ten seconds after the game last wrote to it
and again on the way out.␀Arcade␀␀One 1980 board, and everything that ran on it.␀␀Runs the Namco Pac-Man board, and a great many games shipped on it:
Pac-Man and Puck Man, the Ms. Pac-Man bootlegs that ran on an
unmodified board, Crush Roller, Ponpoko, Eyes, Piranha, Mr. TNT,
Jump Shot and a long tail of bootleg reskins. No daughterboard is
emulated; every game here is one the plain board ran.

The distinct games are starred, because most of the list is reskins.

An arcade machine's ROMs are several chips. mkarcade.py --all packs the
romsets you have into .arc files for /arcade on the card. No ROMs come
with this device.

The cabinet's monitor stands on its side, so the picture does too. FULL
fills the panel's height exactly and expects a USB gamepad; 2x leaves
room for the on-screen controls.

SELECT puts a coin in. START begins the game. Hold both to come back.

2P is the cabinet's second start button. Ms. Pac-Man Plus keeps its
speed-up there and its invincibility on START. Neither is a DIP switch.

Most of these games have no button at all, and on those a gamepad's
four face buttons are a second stick: X up, B down, Y left, A right.
Either hand will do. The games that do use a button keep it.

Ms. Pac-Man was never a board but a kit that plugged into one, swapping
its own encrypted program in and out. That kit is emulated, so the real
game and its speedup hack run here, not only the bootlegs.␀␀␀SNES␀␀␀␀Super Nintendo, if you brought the core.␀␀␀␀Runs SNES ROMs from /snes on a FAT32 microSD card.

The emulator core is not part of this project and is not shipped with
it - this entry exists because whoever built this firmware fetched the
core themselves. See third_party/snes9x/README.md.

Cartridges with a Super FX chip do not run. Games with battery-backed
saves keep them in a .srm file beside the ROM.

2x TOUCH plays with the on-screen pad, which has no X, Y or shoulder
buttons - a real SNES game usually wants them, so 3x GAMEPAD and a USB
controller is the better way to play. Hold SELECT and START to come
back.␀␀␀␀Joshua␀␀Tic-tac-toe. Would you like to play a game?␀Tic-tac-toe on WOPR's terminal, as the film played it. Touch
TIC TAC TOE when it is offered, then choose how many players.

ZERO: the computer plays itself, faster and faster, until it learns
the lesson. ONE: you are X and move first; the computer plays
perfectly, so a draw is the best you can do. TWO: two people take
turns on the one screen.

X always moves first. Tap a square to play it. A finished game
shows the result and returns to the start on its own.

MENU and ? sit in the corners of the terminal screens, between
games; the board itself has no controls on it.␀␀␀The 1993 one. Bring your own WAD.␀␀␀Runs Doom from a .wad file in /doom on a FAT32 microSD card: doom.wad,
doom1.wad (the shareware episode), doom2.wad, plutonia.wad, tnt.wad,
or Freedoom's freedoom1.wad and freedoom2.wad. The file's NAME is how
the game is told apart, so keep the original names. An add-on such as
SIGIL plays on the doom.wad or doom2.wad beside it: pick the add-on.
No WAD is shipped with this device.

TOUCH PAD plays with the on-screen pad: the D-pad walks and turns, FIRE
fires, USE opens doors and works switches (and picks in the menus),
WEAPON steps to the next weapon, MENU opens the game's own menu. FULL
fills the height and expects a USB gamepad, whose L and R strafe. You
always run. The sidebar there has the volume and this page.

Hold SELECT and START together to come back here; the game waits where
it was. Its saves and settings file go in /doom on the card.␀␀hs_sol_1␀␀␀␀hs_sol_3␀␀␀␀hs_sud_0␀␀␀␀hs_sud_1␀␀␀␀hs_sud_2␀␀␀␀hs_sud_3␀␀␀␀hs_mine_0␀␀␀hs_mine_1␀␀␀hs_mine_2␀␀␀no data␀too short␀␀␀not an .arc file␀␀␀␀unknown arcade board␀␀␀␀too much program ROM␀␀␀␀too many vector fixups␀␀unknown add-on board␀␀␀␀made by a different version of mkarcade␀wrong amount of ROM in it␀␀␀/sfx/%s.wav␀sfx %s: %s
